Argument notes
--------------
filetime: utimensat flags. filestat: kind 0 stat, 1 lstat, 2 fstat.
fileowner: fchownat flags. fifocreate: requested mode. pathcheck: reserved 0.
diskwalk: reserved 0, FTS entry type (zero at terminal return), errno.
  Entry errno is read only for FTS_ERR, FTS_DNR and FTS_NS; terminal errno
  is inherited from fts_read after the caller clears errno. No entry paths.
diskspace: reserved 0. sysctltool: write-buffer-present flag and MIB length.
limittool: resource number. signalall/signaltool: target pid and signal.
logsend operation: requested length, signed send result, errno.
logsend local: priority on both sites; void API completion, not delivery.
daemonwait: change count and event capacity, returned event count, errno.
envdir: reserved 0. configquery: sysconf key and signed long value.
cpusettool: operation kind (0 read, 1 write), level and selector.
timeoutsig operation: pid and signal; reaper: procctl command/result/errno.
pathresolve: reserved 0, boolean success, errno. sleepwait: reserved 0.
tempcreate: kind (0 file, 1 directory), boolean success, errno.

An ENOENT in pathcheck can be acceptable to the utility; syscall errors do
not necessarily mean command failure. Signals numbered zero check access
and existence. An interrupted nanosleep/kevent may be retried by the caller.
No duration is calculated in C: start/done timestamps allow latency analysis
without unconditional timing calls. No new probe runs in a signal handler,
early boot, an interrupt filter, DTrace itself, or an allocator internals path.
ENABLED guards suppress argument evaluation, including FTS field reads, when
inactive. Trace wrappers preserve errno. Enabled traces still have a cost;
per-entry traversal/metadata and event-wait probes may be high volume.

Categories and limits
---------------------
P0: operation lifecycle, latency, syscall errors and interrupted waits.
P1: temporary resources/FIFOs, filesystem I/O, traversal, event waits and
    logging submission/network sends.
P2: ownership/limits/affinity/signal/sysctl decision outcomes through errno.
This is diagnostic instrumentation, not a tamper-resistant security audit.
No invented deadlock/health thresholds or extra per-call counters are added.

