The existing bsdinstruments Makefile discovers profiles/*.d and installs them
under /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles in the bsdinstruments base package.
All ten new entries were verified in the 375-entry install catalog. Utility
Makefiles use the existing MK_DTRACE switch and build native provider objects
into the normal PROG; disabled builds contain none of these providers. No
new standalone executable or external package is required. No installation
or package publication was performed.

Profiles aggregate elapsed microseconds and counts by operation and errno
(or local log priority), using per-pid/tid depth and timestamps. They require
starting before the workload; loss of events invalidates pairing. They make
no pointer/string reads. PID filtering selects the executing process; child
processes require their own selection or an unfiltered profile.

Reproduction and validation
---------------------------
Artifacts: /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-tenth-validation
Prepared sysroot: /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/tmp
Use the maintained coverage-tenth-{base,check,guards,tests,smoke}.py runners
from tools/test/system-tracing; source/object writes require normal OS access.
The base runner uses all logical CPUs (-j16 on this host). It builds only
these programs, not world, and uses the development build configuration.

  40 native utility builds: 20 with MK_DTRACE=yes and 20 with no.
  20 linked executable DOF inspections: argument count, actual probe offsets
     and enabled offsets; provider absence in all 20 disabled counterparts.
  20 offline profile compilations: each profile normal and PID/duration
     filtered, with selector/argument checks and base install selection.
  40 header guard/errno checks: fake providers intentionally corrupt errno;
     active, inactive and compiled-out argument evaluation are checked.
  70 isolated behavior checks, exercising all 20 components in both modes.
  18 existing ATF suite builds; 138 cases passed, 40 root/mount cases skipped,
     two existing touch timestamp cases reported expected_failure.
     One disabled limits CPU-time check measured 2.99 seconds against its
     3.00-second minimum; the isolated recheck passed. The original failure
     and output are retained in results.json under prior_attempt.

Behavior tests use isolated files, read-only sysctl queries, private loopback
logging, signal-zero checks and child-only timeout/limit operations. The
actual daemon objects are relinked with daemon(3) replaced to stay foreground;
a short-lived child writes a marker and supervisor pidfile removal is checked.
The supervisor intentionally exits 1 even after normal completion; the test
checks this existing behavior. No persistent service is started. No system
settings, modules, mounts, upgrades or boot state are changed.

Known limits: /dev/dtrace is unavailable, so no live firing or overhead
measurement is claimed. Offline -Z compilation alone cannot prove provider
presence; independent source contracts and linked DOF checks address that
limit. These are amd64 native builds, not cross-architecture/world/rescue
validation. Privileged mutation/error paths and local syslog delivery are not
comprehensively exercised. The unchanged root-only regressions are skipped.

The manifest hashes the exact reviewed source/profile/test/report snapshot.
The session-only patch preserves preexisting changes and is reverse-checked
against the current tree. No unrelated edits are committed or reverted.
